Renderer benchmark targets

GPU-facing benchmarks synchronize through Horse's backend-neutral Renderer::waitIdle() before recording wall time, so CPU command submission is not mistaken for completed GPU frame time. Median is the primary comparison; mean is printed as a noise indicator.

Forward+ lighting
  c run forward-plus-benchmark

  128 point + 128 spot lights.
  Compares the original full-light loop against Forward+ with alternating sample order.

  2026-09-19 MacBook Air M2 / Metal, 2560x1440 framebuffer:
    full loop   101.739 ms median
    Forward+     41.881 ms median
    delta       -59.858 ms
    speedup      58.83%

  Decision:
    Keep tiled Forward+.
    Do not add depth slices from this result; 2D tiles already remove most of the many-light cost.

Volumetrics
  c run volumetrics-benchmark

  Sweeps Low / Medium / High / Ultra.
  Compares Volumetrics disabled vs enabled for every quality level.

  2026-09-19 MacBook Air M2 / Metal, 2560x1440 framebuffer after shared TLAS/BLAS migration:
    Low       16.676 ms enabled / 16.673 ms disabled median
    Medium    16.583 ms enabled / 16.767 ms disabled median
    High      16.572 ms enabled / 16.878 ms disabled median
    Ultra     56.785 ms enabled / 16.753 ms disabled median

  The ~16.8 ms floor is consistent with VSYNC and prevents precise sub-frame overhead measurement for Low/Medium/High. Ultra clearly exceeds that floor by ~40.0 ms.

  Decision:
    Keep the existing march/blur architecture for Low/Medium/High.
    Do not start froxel migration from this result.
    Ultra remains an explicitly expensive full-resolution quality mode.

Local shadows
  c run shadow-benchmark

  8 point + 8 spot shadow lights over 2048 triangles at Medium quality.
  Reports the cold dirty frame, steady cached frames, and one-light invalidation cost.

  2026-09-19 MacBook Air M2 / Metal, 2560x1440 framebuffer:
    cold dirty frame       52.424 ms
    cached                 16.815 ms median
    one-light invalidated  16.937 ms median
    invalidation delta      0.121 ms

  The cached measurements also sit near the VSYNC floor, but the cold-vs-cached gap and tiny single-light invalidation delta show the dirty-layer cache is doing its job.

  Decision:
    Keep the current local shadow array + dirty-layer cache.
    Do not add a dedicated local-light atlas from this result.

GI / acceleration CPU
  c run gi-benchmark

  If Assets/Sponza/sponza.obj exists locally, it is used automatically.
  Otherwise the benchmark falls back to a deterministic 8192-triangle synthetic grid.

  The earlier leaf-size sweep established 8 triangles per BLAS leaf as the default:

  2026-09-19 MacBook Air M2 synthetic 8192-triangle grid:
    leaf 1
      nodes          16383
      depth             14
      topology       31.406 ms CPU
      refit           2.965 ms median CPU
      probe           0.008 ms median CPU

    leaf 4
      nodes           4095
      depth             12
      topology       25.028 ms CPU
      refit           2.702 ms median CPU
      probe           0.008 ms median CPU

    leaf 8
      nodes           2047
      depth             11
      topology       22.463 ms CPU
      refit           2.675 ms median CPU
      probe           0.008 ms median CPU

  After TLAS/BLAS migration, the same synthetic scene measured:
    rigid acceleration  1 BLAS / 1 instance / 8192 unique triangles
    nodes               2048
    depth                 12
    initial topology    22.820 ms CPU
    transform/TLAS      0.019 ms median CPU
    probe               0.010 ms median CPU

  Decision from the measurements:
    Horse BLAS leaves use 8 triangles.
    Relative to leaf 1 this cuts node count by 87.5%, topology build by about 28.5%, and the TLAS architecture reduces transform-only synchronization from roughly 2.7-3.0 ms to roughly 0.02 ms on the synthetic scene.
    Closest-hit traversal is near-child-first and occlusion uses a dedicated any-hit early-out path.

  Current architecture:
    Rigid world meshes use reusable mesh-local BLAS data plus an instance TLAS.
    Moving a rigid instance updates TLAS/instance state without rebuilding its mesh BLAS.
    Skinned/deformed geometry remains on the world-space SceneCache fallback.
    GI and Volumetrics consume one shared CPU acceleration state.
    Volumetrics uploads that shared state into the same flat TLAS/BLAS representation for GPU shadow traversal.
    The GPU upload cache remains Volumetrics-owned; CPU geometry ownership is shared.

  The benchmark reports:
    initial BLAS/TLAS build cost,
    unique BLAS triangles,
    BLAS and instance counts,
    combined acceleration node/depth statistics,
    transform-only TLAS update cost,
    probe update cost.

  Synthetic fallback also runs a shared-BLAS scaling sweep with 1 / 16 / 64 / 256 model copies. Each copy is parented at a different world transform, so the sweep measures TLAS growth while the mesh BLAS remains reusable. For every scale it reports BLAS count, instance count, nodes/depth, initial build cost, transform-only TLAS cost and probe cost.

  To measure another real GAME model:
    c build gi-benchmark
    ./build/debug/gi-benchmark "/absolute/path/to/model.gltf"

  Remaining GI decision:
    use the multi-instance sweep plus at least one representative GAME scene before deciding whether a baked/static-probe path is worth adding. Do not add another GI representation without that evidence.
